/* SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
   SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

   The editor's design tokens, layered over the engine's shipped `@theme`.

   ⚠ **This file declares no colours of its own, and that is the entire point.** Every colour below
   is a `var(--…)` reference to a custom property `EditorTheme.vcss` already puts on the root, so
   there is one palette and not two: `bg-surface` and the hand-written
   `panel { background: var(--surface); }` read the same declaration, a user's theme file overriding
   `--surface` through `ThemeService` moves both, and `root.dark` moves both again when the theme is
   toggled. A theme that copied the hex out of `EditorTheme` would be a second palette that agreed
   with the first until the day somebody edited one of them.

   ⚠ **A token whose value names itself is an alias, and the generator will not emit it.** Every
   entry here is of that shape — `--radius-row: var(--radius-row)` says "whatever the document
   already declares under that name" — so `ThemeTokens.RootRuleFor` skips it rather than writing a
   `root` rule that shadows the real value with a self-reference. That rule is what makes the
   pointer idiom safe; without it every radius in the editor would go square.

   ⚠ **The colour names are the token names, verbatim**, which is what makes the mapping checkable
   by eye — and the price is that the family prefix and the token name sometimes both say the same
   word: `text-text-muted` is the muted text colour, and `border-border` is the hairline. Renaming
   either half to read better is how a second vocabulary starts.

   ⚠ **This file used to be `vixen.ui.yaml` and the YAML is deleted rather than ported.** It already
   declared nothing but `var()` pointers into a stylesheet, which is `@theme` with a translation
   step in front of it — and the two limitations it carried in its own comments were both the same
   bug, a token store holding parsed values where the theme holds references. Both are gone below.

   **An opacity modifier on one of these works.** `bg-accent/50` emits
   `color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 50%, transparent)`, which never takes the colour apart.
   `opacity-50` on the element is still the right answer when the *whole* element should be
   translucent; the modifier is for when only the fill is. */

@theme {
    /* ── What the editor does not want from the engine's defaults ────────────
       Both of these are `initial`, which is v4's own way of emptying a namespace, and both are
       decisions rather than tidiness.

       ⚠ **The colours, because the editor's palette is a designed thing and a superset of it is
       not.** `EditorTheme.vcss`' remarks are the argument: near-black tool-window density, four
       surfaces whose depth comes from luminance steps rather than borders, and a hairline that is
       *darker* than the surface it edges. Twenty-six general-purpose hue ramps sitting beside that
       would be twenty-six ways to write a colour that is not in it, all of them resolving cleanly.
       A game wants the opposite and gets the opposite, which is the whole point of shipping the
       palette as a default rather than as a floor.

       ⚠ **The breakpoints, because a tool window is not a page.** A panel is sized by the dock that
       holds it and not by the display, so `md:` would be asking the wrong question — the answer a
       panel wants is its own width, which a media query cannot see. Cleared rather than left
       available and misleading. Container queries are the mechanism that actually answers it; see
       docs/plan/43 § D3. */
    --color-*: initial;
    --breakpoint-*: initial;

    /* ⚠ Four, and it used to be two. The editor's chrome was drawn on a two-pixel rhythm and a base
       of 2 made every measurement in the sheet a whole number of steps; the chrome is being redone,
       and an engine whose spacing base differs from Tailwind's by a factor of two means every `p-4`
       a Tailwind author writes is half the size they expect. The exception goes with the rhythm
       that justified it. */
    --spacing: 4px;

    /* ── The palette ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The four surfaces the whole depth model is made of. See `EditorTheme.vcss`' remarks: depth
       here comes from luminance steps rather than from borders, and these are the steps. */
    --color-workspace: var(--workspace);
    --color-surface: var(--surface);
    --color-surface-sunken: var(--surface-sunken);
    --color-surface-raised: var(--surface-raised);
    --color-surface-hover: var(--surface-hover);

    /* The hairline, which is *darker* than the surface it edges — a seam rather than a bevel. */
    --color-border: var(--border);
    --color-border-active: var(--border-active);

    --color-text: var(--text);
    --color-text-muted: var(--text-muted);

    --color-accent: var(--accent);
    --color-accent-text: var(--accent-text);
    --color-accent-soft: var(--accent-soft);
    --color-accent-deep: var(--accent-deep);

    --color-danger: var(--danger);
    --color-warning: var(--warning);

    /* The parts a control draws itself out of, which are tokens because a slider, a scrollbar and a
       progress bar are the same three colours arranged differently. */
    --color-track: var(--track-color);
    --color-fill: var(--fill-color);
    --color-thumb: var(--thumb-color);
    --color-selection: var(--selection-color);
    --color-caret: var(--caret-color);

    /* Chrome is the strip around the working surface — menu bar, toolbar, status bar — and it is its
       own ramp because it sits *on* the desk rather than being one of the cards laid on it. */
    --color-chrome: var(--chrome);
    --color-chrome-sunken: var(--chrome-sunken);
    --color-chrome-text: var(--chrome-text);

    /* ⚠ Three shades each, and the soft/strong pair is not decoration. A green button hovered with
       the generic neutral grey reads as the colour draining out of it at the one instant it most
       needs to look live. */
    --color-play: var(--play);
    --color-play-soft: var(--play-soft);
    --color-play-strong: var(--play-strong);
    --color-pause: var(--pause);
    --color-pause-soft: var(--pause-soft);
    --color-pause-strong: var(--pause-strong);
    --color-stop: var(--stop);
    --color-stop-soft: var(--stop-soft);
    --color-stop-strong: var(--stop-strong);

    --color-marquee-edge: var(--marquee-edge);
    --color-marquee-fill: var(--marquee-fill);

    /* ── Radii ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       ⚠ **This is the entry the old YAML could not write, and the reason it could not is gone.**
       Its comment read: "`radius` is missing on purpose … `ThemeTokens.Radius` is a
       `Dictionary<string, float>`: it parses numbers, so `var(--radius-row)` is rejected with a
       diagnostic rather than stored." So the editor had three radii on the root, no radius tokens,
       and the only way to round a row was an arbitrary value naming the variable inline. `Radius`
       holds text now, which is what every other namespace already did, these three are ordinary
       tokens, and `rounded-row` resolves.

       ⚠ **That old spelling is described here and no longer written out, which is not fastidiousness
       about prose.** `CandidateScanner` scans `**/*.vcss` and deliberately parses nothing, so a class
       name typed into a comment is indistinguishable from one typed into a `class` attribute: for as
       long as the bracket form sat in this paragraph, every build of `Vixen.Editor.Ui` emitted a rule
       for it, and anyone reading the generated sheet saw the editor apparently still using the
       workaround this very comment exists to say it had stopped using. One rule of the twenty-five
       was a sentence about history.

       The engine's own `--radius-xs … --radius-4xl` are deliberately *not* cleared: they are v4's
       scale, they collide with nothing here, and a new panel wanting `rounded-md` should have it. */
    --radius-panel: var(--radius-panel);
    --radius-control: var(--radius-control);
    --radius-row: var(--radius-row);

    /* Shadows are strings, so the elevation token survives the round trip whole — which is also the
       shape a shadow token should have: offset, blur and alpha are chosen together to read as one
       height above the surface. */
    --shadow-elevation: var(--elevation);

    /* ⚠ `EditorTheme.vcss` declares no font-size tokens — it writes sizes inline, mostly as `em`
       against the host's — so this is a new scale rather than a mirror of an existing one, and it is
       kept to the four sizes the chrome actually uses. It overrides four steps of v4's scale and
       leaves the other nine alone. The line height is a length here rather than a ratio, which is
       CSS's own distinction and not this file's. */
    --text-xs: 10px;
    --text-xs--line-height: 14px;
    --text-sm: 11px;
    --text-sm--line-height: 16px;
    --text-base: 12px;
    --text-base--line-height: 18px;
    --text-lg: 14px;
    --text-lg--line-height: 20px;

    /* ⚠ No `--dark-mode`. Neither strategy is what the editor does: its dark theme is `ThemeService`
       putting a `dark` class on the *root* and the tokens in `EditorTheme.vcss` being redeclared
       under `root.dark`. A `dark:` variant would be a second mechanism selecting the same thing.
       Write `bg-surface` and let the token move. */
}
